8. Database Integration
The application uses the pg package to connect to PostgreSQL through a connection pool. The database connection reads DATABASE_URL from the environment. API routes query the cpen208 schema directly. The outstanding-fees route calls cpen208.get_outstanding_fees(), so the application reuses the database function created in Project 1 rather than duplicating the calculation in TypeScript.
9. Input Validation and Error Handling
The API validates required fields before sending database requests. It checks conditions such as missing student IDs, missing course codes, invalid enrolment statuses, invalid payment amounts, and duplicate records. The fee API also maps common PostgreSQL errors to clearer messages, including duplicate payment references, invalid payment amounts, and missing foreign-key records.
10. Assumptions and Configuration
• The application uses the PostgreSQL database named cpen208.
• DATABASE_URL stores the database connection string.
• JWT_SECRET stores the secret used to sign sessions.
• The local .env.local file remains private and should not be committed to GitHub.
• The .env.example file documents the required environment variables without exposing private credentials.
11. Running the Application
• Create the PostgreSQL database and run the Project 1 schema and seed scripts.
• Set DATABASE_URL and JWT_SECRET in the local environment file.
• Run npm install to install the project dependencies.
• Run npm run dev to start the Next.js development server.
• Open the application at http://localhost:3000.
• Register an account, log in, and use the protected dashboard.
12. Verification
The source code contains separate API routes for the main Project 1 functions and includes the required registration, login, and dashboard pages. The generated Next.js build files in the submitted project also show that the application has been built locally. The API routes include validation and database error handling to support normal and invalid input cases.
